WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.901 --> 00:00:02.300
-Welcome to the show.

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:03.667
Gabrielle, do you --
do you --

00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:06.234
When you see your book out,
what do you --

00:00:06.234 --> 00:00:08.734
Do you go, "Oh, yeah, that's me.
That's my book."

00:00:08.734 --> 00:00:09.901
-You know what
the coolest thing is?

00:00:09.901 --> 00:00:12.200
Like, the Book Club seal.
Like, that's slick.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.901
-You like that?
-Don't you?

00:00:13.901 --> 00:00:15.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.501 --> 00:00:17.033
-It's not bad.
I worked on that.

00:00:17.033 --> 00:00:18.868
I wanted to make it classy
and look good.

00:00:18.868 --> 00:00:20.300
-No, it's so classy.
-It is?

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.634
-I think it's -- it feels
like it's been around forever,

00:00:21.634 --> 00:00:23.100
not like we're the first ones.
-Right?

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.367
-You know,
but I am the first one.

00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:25.634
-But you are the first one.
Exactly, Gabrielle.

00:00:25.634 --> 00:00:27.033
-Yeah.
-Thank you.

00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:29.100
I love this book,

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.801
"Tomorrow, and Tomorrow,
and Tomorrow."

00:00:30.801 --> 00:00:33.267
I want to get into it.
But I wanted to show something,

00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:36.334
because you didn't --
no one starts writing novels.

00:00:36.334 --> 00:00:37.834
They start -- everyone has --

00:00:37.834 --> 00:00:39.934
-Well, as a baby, I just came
out and started writing novels.

00:00:39.934 --> 00:00:41.968
-Just started writing books.
-That's how that works.

00:00:41.968 --> 00:00:45.000
-No, but talk to me about,
you were a high school --

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.734
was it a concert critic?
What were you?

00:00:46.734 --> 00:00:48.234
-[ Laughs ]
-A music critic?

00:00:48.234 --> 00:00:51.300
-I was a music critic for the
Fort Lauderdale "Sun Sentinel."

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.434
-Yeah.
-They had a --

00:00:52.434 --> 00:00:53.701
-Whoo!
-Wow, okay.

00:00:53.701 --> 00:00:54.868
Wave for Fort Lauderdale.
-Yeah, one person, yeah.

00:00:54.868 --> 00:00:55.968
[ Laughter ]
Yeah, yeah.

00:00:55.968 --> 00:00:57.601
-Yeah, they had a special
teen section.

00:00:57.601 --> 00:00:59.400
And I went
to a Guns n' Roses concert,

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.467
and when I got home that night,
there was a really mean review,

00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:04.901
insulting to Axl,
insulting to the band.

00:01:04.901 --> 00:01:06.300
-No.
-You know?

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.701
And so, I wrote an angry letter
to that newspaper.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:13.167
And I ended up getting hired to
review, like, concerts and CDs.

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:14.734
-Like, a teen consultant...
-Yes.

00:01:14.734 --> 00:01:16.033
-Critic.
This is --

00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:17.400
I mean, this is --
Cameron Crowe, man.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.901
This is how everyone --
But I love the bottom of this.

00:01:19.901 --> 00:01:22.100
It says here's --
It says, "If South Florida

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.567
ever sees a concert
of this caliber again,

00:01:24.567 --> 00:01:26.968
that concert would
defy the laws of nature.

00:01:26.968 --> 00:01:30.834
After all, lightning never
strikes the same place twice."

00:01:30.834 --> 00:01:32.734
-[ Laughs ]
-Well-written.

00:01:32.734 --> 00:01:34.234
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:34.234 --> 00:01:36.801
-And stick up for Axl and Slash
and Duff and everybody.

00:01:36.801 --> 00:01:38.901
Guns n' Roses.

00:01:38.901 --> 00:01:41.067
Well, "Tomorrow, and Tomorrow,
and Tomorrow,"

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:42.200
big hit, by the way.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.334
You've been on
"The New York Times"

00:01:43.334 --> 00:01:45.400
bestseller list, top-10 list,
for now --

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.000
since it came out, actually.
-Yeah.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.501
-You were actually --
I heard that you're very excited

00:01:48.501 --> 00:01:50.033
that it ca--
it opened at number three.

00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:52.033
-Well, I just want to say
that I would've been fine

00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:53.901
with it being number one.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:53.901 --> 00:01:56.968
But I was -- I was really
excited with three, as well,

00:01:56.968 --> 00:01:59.400
because there was three
"tomorrows" in the title.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.567
-Okay.
-That makes sense, right?

00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:01.834
-Yeah, tomorrow, tomorrow,
tomorrow.

00:02:01.834 --> 00:02:03.901
-And I was also then excited
when it was number five,

00:02:03.901 --> 00:02:05.200
because there's
two "ands" there,

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.000
so you get "Tomorrow,
and Tomorrow, and Tomorrow."

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.200
[ Light laughter ]
-Okay.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.367
-And when it was number seven,
still excited,

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:12.267
because there are actually
two commas after each tomorrow.

00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:13.734
-All right. Ridiculous.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.734 --> 00:02:16.133
That's --
[ Both laugh ]

00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:19.601
That's ridiculous.
But how do we describe the book?

00:02:19.601 --> 00:02:21.467
-Whenever anybody asks me
to synopsize the book,

00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:22.701
I basically panic,

00:02:22.701 --> 00:02:24.234
because I think the book's
pretty complicated.

00:02:24.234 --> 00:02:25.567
-Is it, really?
-So I've started saying,

00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:28.300
"It's just about love, art,
video games, and time,"

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:29.734
which I know
sounds really vague,

00:02:29.734 --> 00:02:33.067
so then I add,
"Well, it's about these two kids

00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:35.601
who meet as children,

00:02:35.601 --> 00:02:39.434
and they spend the next 30 years
playing games and making games."

00:02:39.434 --> 00:02:41.133
You know?
And it's sort of about,

00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:43.734
"What if the person
who's the most important person

00:02:43.734 --> 00:02:45.701
in your life wasn't
any of the usual suspects?

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:47.567
What if it wasn't, like,
your wife or your kids?

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:50.801
What if it really was your
colleague and your friend?

00:02:50.801 --> 00:02:53.267
And I think the book reflects
my belief that

00:02:53.267 --> 00:02:56.601
a great love story can also
be a great friendship story.

00:02:56.601 --> 00:02:58.234
-Yeah.
See? Look at that.

00:02:58.234 --> 00:02:59.300
Right now,
everyone's like, "Yeah."

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.067
[ Cheers and applause ]
It is that good.

00:03:01.067 --> 00:03:04.834
I love --
So, I'm reading it, and I go,

00:03:04.834 --> 00:03:07.100
okay, "tomorrow,
and tomorrow, and tomorrow,"

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:11.300
I know is from "Macbeth,"
but what is the connection

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.467
for you between "tomorrow,
and tomorrow, and tomorrow"?

00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:16.267
-Well, I have to say, when you
did your little video about it,

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:18.400
you had the title exactly right.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.801
So, while it does
come from one of the bleakest

00:03:20.801 --> 00:03:22.367
speeches in all
of Shakespeare...

00:03:22.367 --> 00:03:23.767
Yeah.
-...you know, it's Macbeth's

00:03:23.767 --> 00:03:26.100
act five soliloquy,
and nothing good happens

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.167
in act five of "Macbeth"
to anybody.

00:03:28.167 --> 00:03:31.067
But the character who, like,
cites it in my novel,

00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:32.634
he actually
finds great hope in it,

00:03:32.634 --> 00:03:34.801
the idea that every day
we're alive

00:03:34.801 --> 00:03:36.300
is a chance to start again.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.434
And conveniently for me, he also
finds a metaphor for video games

00:03:39.434 --> 00:03:43.734
with their infinite lives and
infinite chances at redemption,

00:03:43.734 --> 00:03:45.434
which I think is something
you really hit upon

00:03:45.434 --> 00:03:47.434
when you were
guessing what the title was.

00:03:47.434 --> 00:03:49.167
-Yeah. I did --
Great.

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:50.767
So, I did say that, yeah.
-You did say that.

00:03:50.767 --> 00:03:52.501
[ Laughter ]
-Thank you.

00:03:52.501 --> 00:03:54.968
Yeah, because I was thinking,
I do love games,

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:57.367
and I love
all the references to gaming.

00:03:57.367 --> 00:03:59.133
We were just talking backstage.

00:03:59.133 --> 00:04:01.534
Our parents both worked
at IBM growing up.

00:04:01.534 --> 00:04:03.601
-Yes.
-By the way,

00:04:03.601 --> 00:04:05.667
I had a great experience
with IBM.

00:04:05.667 --> 00:04:06.868
-Yeah, no.
-They were really great.

00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:09.033
-Like, people have no idea
how IBM was, like,

00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:10.734
your whole life
if your parents worked there.

00:04:10.734 --> 00:04:13.133
There were Christmas parties,
there was, like, Family Funday.

00:04:13.133 --> 00:04:14.701
-Family Day. Yes!
-You know?

00:04:14.701 --> 00:04:16.367
-Family Day was great!
-And so we were thinking

00:04:16.367 --> 00:04:18.467
maybe we went to the same ones.
-Yeah, maybe.

00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:20.367
We could've been there together.
-But we aren't --

00:04:20.367 --> 00:04:22.234
So, we're basically
related through IBM.

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:24.601
-Yes, we are!
We're IBM brother/sister, yeah.

00:04:24.601 --> 00:04:26.334
-Right.
Which you can tell.

00:04:26.334 --> 00:04:28.868
-But there's so many references
there to "Oregon Trail."

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:30.701
-Yeah.
-That's what really got me.

00:04:30.701 --> 00:04:32.200
'Cause there's two friends
that haven't seen each other

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.334
since high school,
and they're yelling

00:04:34.334 --> 00:04:36.701
across the train station.
And he goes, "Sadie, Sadie!"

00:04:36.701 --> 00:04:37.868
And she's not turning around.

00:04:37.868 --> 00:04:39.834
He goes, "Sadie,
you died of dysentery!"

00:04:39.834 --> 00:04:40.968
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:40.968 --> 00:04:42.734
-And they decide
to make a game together.

00:04:42.734 --> 00:04:45.601
-Yeah.
-And that's really interesting.

00:04:45.601 --> 00:04:47.701
'Cause I've never heard of
a love story where you're like,

00:04:47.701 --> 00:04:50.200
"Oh, that's a new take on" --
I've seen every Hallmark movie.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.634
[ Laughter ]
And I go --

00:04:52.634 --> 00:04:55.434
But the games are genius,
when you think about them.

00:04:55.434 --> 00:04:58.400
And then I heard that maybe
they might be in development.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.267
-Well, they made "Emilyblaster,"
as you know,

00:05:00.267 --> 00:05:01.701
so you can play that.
You know?

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:03.133
-Can you explain "Emilyblaster"
to people?

00:05:03.133 --> 00:05:06.234
-Yes. So, it's a game
that my character, Sadie,

00:05:06.234 --> 00:05:08.434
makes in college,
and you shoot fragments

00:05:08.434 --> 00:05:10.601
of the poetry
of Emily Dickinson.

00:05:10.601 --> 00:05:11.934
[ Light laughter ]
So you can go --

00:05:11.934 --> 00:05:14.267
If you've ever wanted to shoot
Emily Dickinson's poetry,

00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:15.901
which, who among us hasn't?
[ Laughter ]

00:05:15.901 --> 00:05:17.300
-I mean, this is --
-Right?

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.167
-Why has this not been invented?
-Why has this not been invented?

00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:20.801
-Exactly.
-You can play that

00:05:20.801 --> 00:05:22.801
at Emilyblaster.com.
But, you know --

00:05:22.801 --> 00:05:23.934
But the other games, yeah,

00:05:23.934 --> 00:05:25.367
like, I would love
to see them get made.

00:05:25.367 --> 00:05:28.267
I'm thinking right now when we
read a book or we watch a movie,

00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:30.467
we want to experience it
in different ways.

00:05:30.467 --> 00:05:33.033
And gaming storytelling
is so different

00:05:33.033 --> 00:05:36.100
than book storytelling
or movie storytelling.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.634
In a way, you get to be a part
of it in this really active way.

00:05:38.634 --> 00:05:40.801
And so I think it would be
awesome if people make games.

00:05:40.801 --> 00:05:42.767
-It already got --
the rights got sold.

00:05:42.767 --> 00:05:44.567
Someone bought this to make
a movie out of this, right?

00:05:44.567 --> 00:05:46.467
-Yes. So...
-Come on.

00:05:46.467 --> 00:05:48.133
[ Cheers and applause ]
Come on.

00:05:48.133 --> 00:05:49.501
I mean, come on!

00:05:49.501 --> 00:05:52.934
Fallon Book Club first.
That's how you do it.

00:05:52.934 --> 00:05:56.067
Gabrielle Zevin, I wanted
to present you with this.

00:05:56.067 --> 00:05:59.167
This is our first ever
Fallon Book Club pin.

00:05:59.167 --> 00:06:00.734
-What?
-Yeah.

00:06:00.734 --> 00:06:01.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:01.868 --> 00:06:03.701
So, that's for you.

00:06:03.701 --> 00:06:05.467
It has the date on it.
-I'm putting it on.

00:06:05.467 --> 00:06:07.167
-Yeah. Can I put it on?
-Please do.

00:06:07.167 --> 00:06:09.200
-Oh, yeah, I'd love to.
Here we go.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:10.868
I don't want to ruin
this jacket here.

00:06:10.868 --> 00:06:12.033
-No, you won't.

00:06:12.033 --> 00:06:13.534
♪♪

00:06:13.534 --> 00:06:14.667
-All right.
There we go, right there.

00:06:14.667 --> 00:06:16.667
Wow, look at the camera.
-Wow, that is sharp.

00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:18.501
And I wore black.
-Look at that, wow.

00:06:18.501 --> 00:06:20.467
[ Cheers and applause ]
That's what I'm talking about.

00:06:20.467 --> 00:06:23.100
Gabrielle Zevin,
congrats on everything!

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:24.667
"Tomorrow, and tomorrow,
and tomorrow."

00:06:24.667 --> 00:06:25.968
You're awesome.
-Thank you.

00:06:25.968 --> 00:06:27.934
-Come back for the next one.
We love you.

00:06:27.934 --> 00:06:30.033
We'll be right back with a
performance from Em Beihold.

00:06:30.033 --> 00:06:31.100
Stick around, everybody.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.133
"Tomorrow, and Tomorrow,
and Tomorrow."

